## Object Oriented Programming

# 1. Land Management System
This module allows for the registration and reporting of different land types: Agricultural, Residential, Commercial, and Industrial. Users interact with a console-based menu to input land details such as land ID, owner name, location, land size, and status. The system applies validation to ensure correct data entry formats (e.g., numeric for ID, alphabetic for names). Each land type can capture additional attributes (like number of units for residential or clearance status for industrial). After registration, a report is generated, and all registered land details are displayed at the end.

# 2. Mission Management System
The mission system simulates military or emergency operations. It allows users to:

Add multiple personnel (validated by ID and name).

Register multiple resources.

Choose a mission type (Recon, Rescue, Combat, Humanitarian).

Enter mission details such as name, ID, start and end dates (validated date format), and status.

Depending on the selected type, specific validation rules are enforced (e.g., Recon missions require at least two personnel). Once data is captured, tasks are assigned, resources allocated, and mission progress is tracked. The system concludes by generating a mission report.

# 3. Nursery School Management System
This is a class-based school simulation consisting of Baby Class, Middle Class, and Top Class. The menu-driven system offers options to assign teachers, enroll students, conduct activities, add assessments (for Top Class only), track progress, and generate reports.

Students and teachers are added using validated input. The design uses polymorphism where shared behaviors (like enrollment or reporting) are defined in a superclass or interface, while class-specific actions (like assessments in Top Class) are handled via class-specific implementations. It provides a basic but robust simulation of a nursery school environment.

Overall Design and Architecture
Each system is well-structured and follows Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles. Input validation, menu-based navigation, and clean encapsulation of class responsibilities are consistently implemented. All three systems use scanner-based input, and each has structured user interaction workflows, ensuring the systems are user-friendly and robust against incorrect inputs.
